WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.860
Let me introduce our
speaker for today.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.720
We are delighted
to have Vivek, who

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.280
is a research scientist at
Google DeepMind, leading

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.980
research at the intersection
of AI, Science, and Medicine.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.600
He is the lead researcher
behind Med-PaLM

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.200
and Med-PaLM 2, the
first AI systems

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.520
to obtain passing
and expert-level

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.680
scores on US medical
license exam, respectively.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.040
Vivek also co-leads project
AMIE, a research program

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.560
aiming to build and democratize
medical superintelligence.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:41.360
And prior to Google, he worked
on multimodal assistant systems

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.840
at Facebook AI
research, and he is also

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.840
part of the faculty for
executive education at Harvard

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:50.160
TH Chan School of Public Health.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.140
Let's welcome him.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.352
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.460
Thank you, Karan Thank you
to the course organizers.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.240
And thank you,
everyone, for coming in

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.760
and sorry for not
starting exactly on time.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.860
But hopefully, this will
be a useful session.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.505
And yeah, today I
want to be telling you

00:01:09.505 --> 00:01:10.880
about some of the
work that we've

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.360
been doing over
the past few years

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.040
at the intersection of
agentic AI systems and science

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.820
and medicine.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.480
The underlying
theme of this work

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.120
is to build out generally
capable and general-purpose AI

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.040
systems that can act as
collaborative partners

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.680
for experts in the scientific
and medical domain,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.960
that is, namely
scientists and doctors.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.360
So the first project I want
to be telling you about

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.880
is this effort
called co-scientist.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.200
So the system that we're
trying to build out over here,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.200
the mission for this work
is to essentially build out

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.880
a collaborative
partner for scientists

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.200
and the hope is that
with this system,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.240
we can really
accelerate the clock

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.000
speed of scientific and
biomedical discoveries

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.320
and really give scientists
superpowers to do

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.400
the work that they are doing.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.360
And it's funny because
this work actually

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.000
has its genesis in
a talk that I gave

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.760
at Stanford a few years back.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:26.300
So I think, as Karan
mentioned, back in 2023,

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.040
we had this paper
published in Nature

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.640
where we introduced this large
language model called Med-PaLM.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.320
So Med-PaLM was one of the first
examples of a medically tuned

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.800
large language model.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.760
So we built out the
system to do well on tasks

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.320
medical question-answering
and summarization.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.520
And back then, it was
one of the first systems

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.280
to do well on this
benchmark, which

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:58.070
was reflective of US medical
license exam-style questions.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.150
So it was one of the first
models to reach passing level

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.450
and then later on a future
version of the system,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.550
reach like expert-level
scores on this benchmark.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.230
So that made headline results.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:13.770
So I think it was like October
2023 when I was at Stanford,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.950
and I was actually giving
a talk on that system.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.350
And at the end of
the talk, there

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:22.870
was this one professor
over here, Dr. Gary Peltz.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.950
And he kind of walked over
to me and my teammate, Tao.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:29.290
And he essentially sent us
like a follow-up email later.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.190
And he explained
that we are using

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.670
this system for medical
question-answering

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.470
and summarization.

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:39.190
But he also thought that you
could use large language models

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:41.950
for another kind of application.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:47.110
So his idea was essentially
that because LLMs

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.830
are trained on a lot of
scientific and medical tokens,

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:55.430
you could actually prompt it
to do hypothesis generation.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:57.070
For example, he
wanted to actually use

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:02.750
it to identify causative gene
factors for rare diseases

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.910
and so on and so forth.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.290
So it was an interesting idea.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:11.830
But back then, we were
still dealing with our PaLM

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.990
class of models over here.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.110
So for those of
you who don't know,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.680
PaLM is the precursor to Gemini.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.930
And I hope that some of you
are at least using Gemini.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.590
Trust me, it's good, maybe
not as good as ChatGPT or--

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:29.145
I'm kidding, but you
guys should try it.

00:04:29.145 --> 00:04:30.770
So it was like the
precursor to Gemini.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.090
And Gemini itself is now
three generations old.

00:04:36.727 --> 00:04:38.310
So at that point of
time, it was still

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.852
struggling to get reliable
answers from the system.

00:04:41.852 --> 00:04:44.310
There would be hallucinations
all over the place and things

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.710
like that.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.190
So the idea that you could
use an LLM-based system

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.990
to do something like
hypothesis generation,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.110
it kind of felt wild,
because hypothesis generation

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.350
and coming up with novel,
creative scientific ideas

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.778
is squarely in the realm
of expert scientists.

00:05:00.778 --> 00:05:03.070
And to think that you could
build out like an AI system

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:07.470
to do that, it felt like
many, many years away.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.310
So I actually went
back to my team

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.350
and I asked, should we do
this project right now?

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:16.510
And the overwhelming
feedback was that we're

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.390
just not ready for it yet.

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.790
But sometimes, the
best projects--

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.870
and I think you probably
all have experienced this.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.003
But for me, certainly,
I think some

00:05:28.003 --> 00:05:29.670
of the best projects
that I've worked on

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.910
are essentially examples
where we actually don't how

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.150
to reach the end
goal, but we still

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.750
decide to just go
ahead and do this.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:41.210
So this was one of those kind
of projects where we did not

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:45.470
know exactly how to clearly
approach this problem,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.790
or what was going to be like
a solution that would lead us

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:50.070
to this end goal?

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.910
It's almost like you
jumping off a cliff,

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:57.270
and you have to figure out
building out a flying machine

00:05:57.270 --> 00:05:59.350
or an airplane on the way down.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.490
So this project is
an example of that,

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.590
where we just decide to
go ahead and do this,

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:06.290
and we just try to, OK,
let's construct some evals.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.450
Let's make some progress.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.790
Let's just see how
far we can push.

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.950
And that's what
we continue to do.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.270
So what we initially
did was just

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.190
using our PaLM-based system.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:19.130
We put together like
an agentic scaffold.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:20.830
And remember, this
is all like 2023.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.190
The word "agent"
was not being used.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.410
But we still put
together this scaffold,

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.490
which had access to some
databases and some tools.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:30.530
And it could go and
retrieve that information.

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.050
It could read that
literature up.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.410
And then we would ask
it to predict, OK,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.350
what are maybe the most likely
genes to cause this rare disease

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.230
variant, and things like that.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.350
And we started doing that.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.430
And we actually even saw
some promising results.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:47.270
So there was this one
particular hypothesis

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:51.670
that this system came up with
that actually Gary went on

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.350
to validate in his lab
using these CRISPR knocking

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.030
experiments on mouse.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.530
And it turned out
to actually work.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.910
And that ended up being like
a peer-reviewed paper that

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:04.230
was published in Advanced
Science a few months back.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.390
So that was like promising.

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:09.030
But in the process of doing
that, we also realized,

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.630
what are the limitations
of using LLMs

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:15.470
in a naive manner for this
hypothesis generation task?

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.990
And if I were to summarize
it or put it crisply,

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:23.630
I would say that when you're
traditionally using LLMs, say,

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:27.470
Gemini or ChatGPT or Claude, I
would argue that these models

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:32.570
are primarily doing
system 1-style thinking.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.550
So they're like coming up
with very fast responses.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:39.850
So they're akin to coming up
with very intuitive responses

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:41.550
without deep thinking.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:45.390
So it's mostly responses with
surface-level correlations

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.530
and pattern-matching
and things like that.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.760
So that's helpful.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.260
But at the same
time, when you think

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:55.260
about the kind of thinking
that is needed for science

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:59.540
and scientific discovery, it's
actually a very different kind

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:00.900
of thinking.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:05.700
So you would notice that it's
actually a lot more slower.

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.140
It's actually more deliberate.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.900
It's rigorous.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.220
And I think you all might
have experienced that,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:14.720
but when you talk to some
of the best scientists,

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:18.260
they'll tell you that they've
had their best ideas when

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:22.280
they've been thinking about a
problem for weeks or months,

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:24.580
sometimes even like years.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.140
So they'll tell you that, I've
always had this thing going on

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:28.320
in the back of my head.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.570
I've been thinking about this.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.777
And suddenly, I had this nice
creative aha moment over here.

00:08:33.777 --> 00:08:35.860
But essentially, the point
is that that's actually

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.500
a very different
kind of thinking.

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:39.482
So it's more like
system 2-style thinking.

00:08:39.482 --> 00:08:40.940
And I think that
thinking is really

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:45.700
like the hallmark of science and
scientific discovery over here.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.460
So the key research
question for us was,

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:51.740
how do we build out these
AI systems that can actually

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:55.260
perform this structured,
rigorous scientific thinking

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.260
that is really the
hallmark of science

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.660
and scientific discovery?

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.300
So that's one framing
of the problem.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.680
Another way to
think about this is,

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.300
if you want to, say,
build out like a truly

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.620
scientific
superintelligent system,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.480
how would you
progress over there?

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.140
How would you quantify progress?

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.580
So for that, I have
this graph over here.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:22.540
And on the y-axis, you'll see
different scientific tasks

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.580
ordered by complexity.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.740
So they range from things
like literature review

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:30.200
and hypothesis generation
to more complex tasks

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.700
like writing a research paper
or coming up with a PhD thesis

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:37.460
to something that's at
the highest levels, which

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:40.140
is coming up with
paradigm shifting theories

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.740
and breakthroughs, say, like the
general theory of relativity.

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.260
And then on the
x-axis, I have the time

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.140
it might take an
average human scientist

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:51.940
to perform these tasks.

00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:54.780
So it can range from
something minutes and hours

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.780
to days, weeks, months, years,
and sometimes even decades,

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:01.880
and lifetimes to really
do paradigm shifting,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:04.100
like research and theories.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.620
And then if you were to start
asking this question, where

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:11.340
do some of the popular
AI models and systems

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.200
that we have around us--

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.300
where do they actually fall
onto this graph or this plot?

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.900
So a very popular
example is AlphaFold.

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:24.660
So if you were to think,
where does AlphaFold

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:26.540
fall on this graph?

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.680
So you'd see that I
have it over here,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:34.340
which is at the extreme
end of the x-axis.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:38.020
So if you ask me like, why
do I have it over there,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:42.320
I would argue that
AlphaFold has, in some ways,

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.660
it's already done
the work that's

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:50.020
equivalent to millions of human
scientists years worth of work.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.380
And then, if you
come and ask me why,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.420
you just have to think
back to the status quo

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:57.380
before AlphaFold existed.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:10:59.980
So before AlphaFold
existed, to get

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.140
to the structure of
a single protein,

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:06.520
that was often an entire PhD's
worth of effort over here.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:09.580
So it would almost take three to
five years of dedicated PhD work

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:12.860
to get to the structure
of a single protein.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.780
And then literally once
AlphaFold, the model

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.660
was developed and the system
came online, literally

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:23.680
overnight, we have predictions
for millions of protein

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:24.740
literally overnight.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:26.440
So if you were to
think about that,

00:11:26.440 --> 00:11:28.980
it's done the work
equivalent to millions

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.660
of years of human
scientists worth of time

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.420
and effort over here.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.440
So that's very cool, obviously.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.380
And it was recognized with the
Nobel Prize for that reason.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.900
But at the same time, if
you look at this graph,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.140
you'll actually notice that
I actually have AlphaFold

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.140
outside of this graph.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:50.300
And the reason for
that is, at least when

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:54.220
I think about a scientific
superintelligent system,

00:11:54.220 --> 00:11:56.620
I actually think about
generality being a very

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:58.940
important property over here.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:01.680
And again, if you
were to ask me,

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.260
I would say that the one thing
that AlphaFold doesn't have

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:07.380
is this property of generality.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.220
So it's like a very
specialized model.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:11.660
So the only inputs
that it recognizes

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:15.900
are protein sequences, and the
only outputs that it can produce

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.260
are protein
structures over here.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.260
So if you were to give
it like any other kind

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:22.500
of scientific problem,
then it wouldn't

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.728
be able to make sense of it.

00:12:24.728 --> 00:12:27.020
So in that sense, it's a
very, very specialized system.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.600
And it's obviously
very, very useful.

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.700
And it solved like
a grand challenge

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:32.140
that dated back decades.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:34.660
So that's why the
system was recognized

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.460
with a Nobel Prize over here.

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:38.180
But at the same
time, it's an example

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:43.260
of a highly capable but a very
specialized system over here.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.320
But if you want a truly super
intelligent system over here,

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:48.380
you would want this
property of generality.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.460
And what do I mean
by generality?

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:54.340
It is just the ability to
take literally any problem

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:57.940
and figure out a way
to make progress on it.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.540
So you may not necessarily solve
it, but what you would want

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.060
is your algorithm to be
able to make sense of it,

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:06.020
understand it, break
down into steps,

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.540
and make reasonable attempts
towards solving them over here.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.180
So that is what I
mean by generality.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.580
So again, I don't
know how many of

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:19.660
you have seen this Thinking Game
documentary, which chronicles

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:22.500
the history of DeepMind,
starting from its founding

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:25.185
to AlphaGo to AlphaFold.

00:13:25.185 --> 00:13:27.060
So if you haven't seen
it, I would definitely

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:30.020
recommend seeing that
documentary over there.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:34.580
And in that documentary,
there's one clip

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:39.500
where they actually show
IBM's Deep Blue machine

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:41.620
taking on Garry Kasparov.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:47.330
So this was all back in 1999.

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:50.690
So obviously, Deep
Blue was able to beat

00:13:50.690 --> 00:13:52.650
Gary Kasparov over there.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.390
But actually, Demis Hassabis
was like the CEO of DeepMind.

00:13:55.390 --> 00:13:57.930
He actually makes
this comment, which

00:13:57.930 --> 00:13:59.650
I thought was quite insightful.

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:02.810
So what Demis says is that he
was actually more impressed

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:08.067
by Garry Kasparov rather than
by Deep Blue in that sequence.

00:14:08.067 --> 00:14:09.650
And the reason for
that is just simply

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:13.130
that Garry Kasparov's brain
was remarkably general.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:14.770
So it was able to
not only compete

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:17.450
with this giant black box
of a machine in this very

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:19.610
complex game like
chess, but it was also

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:22.090
able to do everything
else that humans can do.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:24.630
So Garry could talk
in multiple languages.

00:14:24.630 --> 00:14:25.790
He could appreciate art.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:30.290
He could think about physics
and philosophy and poetry

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:31.090
and whatnot.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.530
So the human brain has
this remarkable property

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:34.650
of generality.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:39.210
And that, in turn,
allows it to do things

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.810
like science, come up
with creative hypotheses

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.970
for complex scientific
problems and things like that.

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.650
So in many ways, you
could even argue and say

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:54.668
that so far in the entire
universe, the only existence

00:14:54.668 --> 00:14:56.210
proof that we have
for a machine that

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.330
is capable of this
kind of generality

00:14:58.330 --> 00:15:01.530
and this kind of hypothesis
generation capability, that's

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.090
actually just the
human brain over here.

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:07.570
So the question
for us is, can we

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:12.090
build out like an AI system
that can mimic this capability?

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:14.890
So that's the core problem.

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.160
So if you were to ask--

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.910
we're talking about
generality over here--

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:20.627
what is the key building block?

00:15:20.627 --> 00:15:23.210
And again, I would argue that
the key building block over here

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:25.890
is actually language
and natural language.

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:28.410
Because if you have
natural language

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.030
as an input/output interface,
much like we humans do,

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:34.930
we might be able to
understand different concepts,

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:36.650
and we might be able
to make sense of it,

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:40.330
and we might be able to solve
different problems over here.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:45.050
So that's a great starting point
because the large language model

00:15:45.050 --> 00:15:46.890
systems that we have
around us, they're

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:49.810
all built on this paradigm
of natural language input

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.330
and output.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:56.130
So if you were to think about
Gemini or Claude or ChatGPT,

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:57.690
I'm sure you're
all talking to it

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:00.110
about a whole bunch of topics.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:02.170
I'm sure many of you
are using to understand

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.570
your lectures, your homeworks,
but also things like physics

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:09.330
and philosophy and poetry and
whatnot, a whole array of topics

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:10.630
that you talk to this.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.410
And these models, obviously,
are able to understand it

00:16:13.410 --> 00:16:14.787
and interpret it.

00:16:14.787 --> 00:16:16.370
So that's a good
property because they

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.130
have this generality notion.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:20.810
So you can post to
it like questions

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:23.010
about any scientific
problem and hopefully

00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:25.930
that they will be able to
understand and make sense of it.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:30.170
But at the same time, maybe even
at least until say a few months

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:33.290
back, I think the
amount of evidence

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:36.330
that we have about these
LLM-based systems, the kind

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.930
of scientific tasks
that they can do,

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.210
I would argue that it's
still very, very early.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:44.090
I'm sure many of you have
been using these deep research

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:48.570
modes of these systems to do
things like evidence synthesis

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:53.410
and literature summarization
and things like that.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.230
But in terms more complex
scientific tasks, say,

00:16:56.230 --> 00:17:00.170
hypothesis generation or
writing research papers,

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:02.330
we've not seen any
evidence of that.

00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:06.569
So in that sense, there's
a lot of dark space

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:08.530
or blank space in this plot.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.970
So there's a lot of room
for us to make progress up

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:13.730
and to the right over here.

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:15.849
So the goal for us with
the co-scientist effort

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:19.089
was simply just that, can we
build our AI systems that are

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:21.260
provably making
progress on this plot up

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.010
and to the right over
here, so essentially

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:28.250
perform more complex scientific
tasks over longer-time horizons?

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:30.930
So hopefully, that framing
is helpful in terms of what

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:34.370
we're trying to accomplish.

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:39.150
So in order to do this, in
order to build out this, say,

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.810
structured scientific
thinking engine,

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:44.770
the approach that
we took actually

00:17:44.770 --> 00:17:47.850
borrows from the
rich lineage that we

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.570
have at Google DeepMind
over the years.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:54.610
So if you go back to AlphaGo,
and so this is the 10-year

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:57.410
anniversary of
AlphaGo over here--

00:17:57.410 --> 00:18:02.010
so you would see that AlphaGo,
the way it was developed,

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:04.670
the key principle was
this idea of self-play.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:09.930
And to state it in a
very simplified manner,

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.370
the key idea was that
you had agents playing

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:15.610
against each other
in this environment,

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:20.050
and depending on the outcome of
the game, which was obviously

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:24.170
this game of Go over
here, the agents

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:26.890
would receive these
reward signals.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:30.130
And then based on the
reward, the winning moves

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:33.937
or the decisions or
the strategies that

00:18:33.937 --> 00:18:35.770
led to the winning moves
that they would get

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:38.400
reinforced in the
weights of the network.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:43.000
And then the ones that,
say, led to the defeats

00:18:43.000 --> 00:18:45.220
would get deprioritized
in some sense.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.080
So that was the key idea.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:50.320
So it's a combination of
self-play with reinforcement

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:51.920
learning and search.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:56.840
And essentially, the beauty
of AlphaGo-- essentially,

00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:59.640
or actually, it was
more like AlphaZero,

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:02.215
was the fact that you could
create this environment.

00:19:02.215 --> 00:19:03.840
And in this environment,
you could just

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.600
have agents go against
each other using

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.400
this self-play setup.

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.700
And this setup actually scales
remarkably with compute.

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:15.440
So there's no human
interference over here.

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.840
You could just start
the agents from scratch.

00:19:17.840 --> 00:19:19.420
You could set up
this environment.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.670
And if you give the right
kind of rewards at the right

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:23.440
time, what you
essentially see is

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:27.080
that these agents keep on
improving over a period of time.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.160
And then at the end
of, say, three months--

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:32.520
that was actually how long like
AlphaZero was trained for--

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.920
the system became
superhuman over here.

00:19:35.920 --> 00:19:39.840
So that's a pretty
remarkable property to have.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.380
Essentially, throw
compute at a problem,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:43.880
and your algorithm
is just so good

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.360
that you don't have to do much.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:47.600
You just let that system
run for a period of time,

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.320
and then it becomes
superhuman at this task.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.380
Isn't that awesome?

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.640
Isn't that wonderful?

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:56.040
So like the question
for us, now how

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:59.080
do we take that approach,
that idea of self-play,

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:02.040
and apply it to more
complex domains over here.

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:05.100
So we started with Go,
and then in 2019 and 2020,

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.320
people showed that you
could have these systems

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.040
with a very similar
approach work in more

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.160
complex environments like
AlphaStar, for example.

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:15.100
But games are useful.

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:18.560
They're useful testbeds for us
to develop learning strategies

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:23.000
and algorithms, but you want to
apply these agents and these AI

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:27.080
systems and develop them for
more complex real-world tasks,

00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:30.120
such as scientific discovery
or in medicine and things

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:31.207
like that.

00:20:31.207 --> 00:20:33.040
So pretty much that's
what we've been doing.

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:37.680
Over the last few
years, the areas

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.880
where we've been applying these
systems, like the complexity

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:42.900
of the board games
and the environments,

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:44.680
that's just become
more and more,

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:49.040
I would say, sophisticated
over here, essentially.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:50.680
So they've kind of
evolved to become

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:53.800
like the realms of medicine
and scientific discovery

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:56.040
and so on and so forth.

00:20:56.040 --> 00:21:00.160
So the way we generalize
the idea of self-play

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:04.280
to scientific reasoning and
scientific discovery over here

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:07.120
is through this notion of
scientific debates and self

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.640
debates.

00:21:08.640 --> 00:21:11.260
So what we have within
our agentic setup--

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.920
and I'll talk about that in
more detail in the next slide--

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:18.040
is essentially like
a team of agents.

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:20.520
And we have these agents
continuously engage

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:23.560
in debates with each
other over here.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.520
So they continuously generate
scientific hypotheses,

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:31.560
but they also refine and
review and critique each other.

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:36.240
And we let this process go
on for a period of time,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.120
and we continuously introduce
net new knowledge and reward

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:41.160
signals.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.080
And using that reward
signals and using

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:45.280
this net new
information, the system

00:21:45.280 --> 00:21:48.200
is able to self-improve
itself towards

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:51.420
better-quality scientific
hypotheses over here.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:52.820
So that's the key idea.

00:21:52.820 --> 00:21:55.400
So we just let this process
of self-debates or scientific

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:58.320
debates happen for
a period of time.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.080
And then the composite
AI system that

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:02.280
emerges from this
multiagent setup

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:06.360
is actually capable of
scientific hypothesis generation

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.240
and the ability to solve
complex scientific tasks

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.720
over long-time
horizons over here.

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:17.560
So hopefully, that's
helpful framing.

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:21.800
And in this slide,
I have a deep dive

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:26.880
of the architecture of this
multiagent setup over here.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.640
So co-scientist, I
would say, is one

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:32.760
of the first examples of a
general-purpose multiagent

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:35.600
system for scientific discovery.

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.620
And again, there's a lot of
text over here, but trust me,

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.320
it's actually quite simple.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:43.200
And as I mentioned
before, the mission

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:46.400
was to build out a collaborative
AI system or a partner

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:48.000
for scientist over here.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:52.080
So we imagine that the human
scientist would always be

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:55.060
at the driver's seat over here.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:58.080
So they would be the one who
would be guiding the system.

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:01.320
So that's how the input/output
interface is set up.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.760
So it's all set up
in natural language.

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.440
So what that means is
that you can literally

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:09.800
post to it any kind of
scientific problem over here.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.760
So the way that happens is
the scientists can come in

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:16.560
and they can specify
this research goal.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:19.720
So this can be quite short,
or it can be quite long.

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.160
So you can add in
all the details

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:23.920
that you want about
the scientific problem

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:25.320
that you want to solve.

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:29.710
And the idea is to
give as many details

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:31.910
as you need to the
system so that it

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:34.270
can do a good job of coming
up with the right kind

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:37.670
of hypotheses or solutions
that we want over here.

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:40.430
So in addition to
the research goal,

00:23:40.430 --> 00:23:42.370
the scientists can
specify, for example,

00:23:42.370 --> 00:23:47.070
what are some of the
constraints that the system must

00:23:47.070 --> 00:23:49.630
satisfy in the solutions
that it generates?

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:54.710
What might be, say,
some axes or rubrics

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:58.350
to, say, rank different
comparable hypotheses over here?

00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:02.950
They can also articulate some of
the preferences that they have.

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:07.990
But not only that much like,
say, your PhD supervisor

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:10.990
might tell you, go and explore
this initial direction over

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:15.010
here, the scientist can say,
OK, this is the problem,

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:17.827
but I think the solution
might lie over here.

00:24:17.827 --> 00:24:19.410
So go and figure
this out essentially.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.230
So they can give the system
initial like seed directions

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:23.870
to explore over here.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.910
And again, they can also
give additional form

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:28.490
of multimodal data over here.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:31.950
So it can be PDFs of
related publications

00:24:31.950 --> 00:24:33.470
and things like that.

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:36.390
It can also be additional
experimental data over here

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:38.480
that you might have
generated in the lab.

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:40.230
So essentially, that
all forms the context

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:42.630
that is needed for
the system to perform

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.473
its hypothesis generation task.

00:24:45.473 --> 00:24:47.890
So that's all structured and
set up as this research goal.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:50.230
And that's the
input to the system.

00:24:50.230 --> 00:24:52.910
And then what the system
does is it kind of does

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:55.330
a bunch of computation.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:57.030
And as I said, this is dynamic.

00:24:57.030 --> 00:25:00.050
So it can go on for as
much time as you want.

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:03.390
So it can go on for say a
few minutes, to a few hours,

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:05.450
to sometimes even
days and weeks,

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:08.350
depending on the complexity
of the problem here.

00:25:08.350 --> 00:25:11.390
And at the end of that what the
system produces is essentially,

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:14.750
I would say, a research
report with a bunch

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:18.070
of hypotheses or
solutions towards solving

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:21.110
the problem that the scientist
had articulated over here.

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:24.010
So again, the interface
is very simple.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:29.070
It's a research goal in and
research proposal or research

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.550
summary out with a bunch
of hypotheses or solutions

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:34.270
to solve the problem.

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:36.730
And then within the
system, as I said,

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:38.710
it's like a multiagent system.

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:40.390
And the way these
agents are configured

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.990
are essentially to
continuously generate

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:45.050
different kinds of
scientific hypotheses,

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:48.430
but also review, critique,
rank, and prioritize them

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:50.710
over a period of time.

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:54.710
And these agents
are all essentially

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:57.630
our latest and greatest like
Gemini models over here.

00:25:57.630 --> 00:26:01.470
And we set them up with
different kinds of strategies

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:02.990
and system prompts.

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:08.070
So we force them to do some
very specific specialized tasks

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:10.950
within this multiagent setup.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:15.030
So again, there's
a bunch of texts,

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:16.470
but I would say
that the simplest

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:18.750
way to think about this
system is to essentially

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:22.070
think about it a computer
program with a while loop.

00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:25.190
So there's one
function or one method

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:27.630
which corresponds to
continuously generating

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:30.350
different scientific
ideas and hypotheses.

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:33.190
There's another function
to review and critique

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:35.230
those hypotheses over here.

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:39.293
There's a third function to rank
and prioritize these hypotheses.

00:26:39.293 --> 00:26:40.710
And then there's
a fourth function

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:43.970
over here, which is to improve
and evolve those hypotheses.

00:26:43.970 --> 00:26:46.790
So it's like a while loop
with these four functions

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:50.110
that are kind of running
asynchronously and continually.

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:54.390
And all these functions are
implemented using agentic LLM

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:57.910
approaches over here.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.750
So as I mentioned,
each of these agents

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:10.110
have their own system
prompt set up over here.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:12.790
So we call this a
library of strategies.

00:27:12.790 --> 00:27:17.150
So we give each
specific agent a set

00:27:17.150 --> 00:27:20.830
of strategies that can select
from to perform like the tasks

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.270
that it has been given.

00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:25.170
So, for example, to
generate scientific ideas,

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.550
one strategy is to go
read all related research

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:30.390
papers to that
given goal, and then

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:32.630
use those research
papers to build up

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:35.450
a foundation of knowledge,
and then use that to propose,

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:38.190
some idea over here.

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.750
Another strategy to come
up with scientific ideas

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:42.830
is to simulate this debate.

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:45.670
So I think some of you
might have also experienced

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:47.590
this where sometimes
you get your best

00:27:47.590 --> 00:27:48.970
ideas by talking to a friend.

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:51.810
So you both start off
with some initial ideas,

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:53.830
but then you have a back
and forth conversation.

00:27:53.830 --> 00:27:56.430
And then what
emerges out of that

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.550
is an idea that is much better
than what you had both initially

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:01.630
to begin with.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:04.210
So we try to simulate that
within an agentic setup

00:28:04.210 --> 00:28:04.710
over here.

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:08.190
We asked the agent to simulate
an interaction between two

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:08.990
experts.

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:11.172
Let it go on for a few
turns of conversation.

00:28:11.172 --> 00:28:12.630
And then at the
end of that come up

00:28:12.630 --> 00:28:15.830
with a scientific idea or
a hypothesis over here.

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:19.830
So essentially, there's hundreds
of difference of strategies

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:21.330
over here, like
within each agent,

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:23.300
including the [INAUDIBLE] agent.

00:28:23.300 --> 00:28:27.860
And in fact, one of the favorite
pastimes of all our team

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:31.140
members is essentially that
they would spend time listening

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:33.780
to these podcasts from,
say, Terence Tao or David

00:28:33.780 --> 00:28:35.140
Deutsch and others.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:37.940
And they would see,
how are these people

00:28:37.940 --> 00:28:39.360
thinking in an abstract manner?

00:28:39.360 --> 00:28:41.720
How are they coming up
with new scientific ideas?

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:44.060
And then they would try
to abstract and summarize

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:47.180
them and implement them
as a prompt within this

00:28:47.180 --> 00:28:49.300
library of strategies
that we have.

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:52.820
And it's a continual evolving
body of knowledge over here,

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:54.560
so you can keep on adding it.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:58.180
And then at test time, you just
have to sample one strategy

00:28:58.180 --> 00:29:03.058
and then use that to generate a
scientific hypothesis over here.

00:29:03.058 --> 00:29:05.100
So similarly, we have a
bunch of these strategies

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:08.380
for the reflection agent,
which is essentially

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.180
doing like this review.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.500
And then the agent that
I want to maybe spend

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:18.420
more time on over here is
actually this ranking agent.

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:21.500
So what this agent does
is it kind of takes

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.980
the hypotheses and the ideas
that have been generated so far.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:29.020
And then it kind of tries
to rank and prioritize them.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:31.740
And the way it does that is
using this debate mechanism

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:34.180
that I mentioned before.

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:37.980
So what it does is it
orchestrates these debate

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:40.380
matches between these ideas.

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:44.560
And then it tries to simulate
a few turns of conversation.

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:48.340
And then essentially, the
goal is to rank these ideas

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:52.540
and say, which one is
better based on the criteria

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:54.860
that the scientist had
articulated initially

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:56.700
in their research goal?

00:29:56.700 --> 00:29:58.020
So that's the setup.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:01.080
So it does these
scientific debates.

00:30:01.080 --> 00:30:04.700
And once you have a few of these
debates set up and run, what

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:07.300
you can then start doing
is you can actually

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:09.420
start like computing
these Elo scores.

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:12.000
So much like in a Go or
in a chess tournament,

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:16.560
you can use the Elo ratings to
and rank players, similarly,

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:18.820
you can take the same
approach over here

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:20.980
and use that to
rank and prioritize

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:23.700
the different scientific
hypotheses over here.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:29.460
And what purpose it serves
is essentially this.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:34.020
So if you were to simply have
only a generation or a review

00:30:34.020 --> 00:30:36.420
agent within the
system, then I think

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:39.940
what that would end up
being is a system that's

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:45.310
generating many, many like good
ideas or decent ideas over here.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.060
But I would argue that
that's actually not

00:30:47.060 --> 00:30:50.500
enough to move the needle in
terms of scientific discovery

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:51.380
over here.

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:54.942
Because when you're
talking to scientists,

00:30:54.942 --> 00:30:56.400
you would see that
they're actually

00:30:56.400 --> 00:30:58.360
not short on ideas over here.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:01.380
The expert scientists
have more ideas

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:04.140
than they have time
and resources for.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:05.940
So if you like
truly building out

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:08.000
a collaborative partner
for these scientists,

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:11.740
then you have to respect their
time and their attention,

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.820
and only surface ideas that
are really worth their time

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:16.080
and their attention over here.

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:19.020
So you have to do a very good
job of prioritizing the outputs

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:21.780
from the system, really
summarizing them,

00:31:21.780 --> 00:31:25.140
and also have this notion
of epistemic humility

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.740
within the system.

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:29.260
And when I say epistemic
humility, what I mean

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:32.460
is that the system should
be able to really convey

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:34.940
when it actually
doesn't about something.

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:36.740
So it should be able
to clearly articulate

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.380
what is its confidence
about a given hypothesis

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:41.700
and also say, what are
the key uncertainties that

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:44.020
need resolving over here?

00:31:44.020 --> 00:31:47.320
So that is what this
ranking agent tries to do.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:50.000
So it essentially tries
to prioritize these ideas.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:53.940
It tries to say, which one is
better and which one's spend--

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:56.860
is worth spending like
time and attention on.

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:59.100
And it also tries
to compute, what

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:02.500
is the uncertainty associated
with each idea over here,

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:05.340
and propagate that
to the scientist.

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:07.602
So that's one purpose
that it serves.

00:32:07.602 --> 00:32:09.060
And then the second
purpose that it

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:12.940
serves is essentially, because
these debates are all happening

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:15.980
in natural language,
what you can do

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:19.140
is you can create
summaries of them.

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:21.140
And then you can put
all of those summaries

00:32:21.140 --> 00:32:25.060
back into the overall
memory of the system.

00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:29.140
And then what happens is the
next time any of these agents

00:32:29.140 --> 00:32:33.920
are doing their work, what
they can do is they can use--

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:36.020
read this summary
from the memory

00:32:36.020 --> 00:32:38.940
and then use that to, say,
come up with better hypotheses

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:42.780
or do a better job at generating
reviews, and so on and so forth.

00:32:42.780 --> 00:32:45.100
And what that helps is it
creates this nice feedback

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:48.820
loop in the system that leads
to a self-improving cycle

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:50.580
over here.

00:32:50.580 --> 00:32:53.000
So that's the crux
of the computation.

00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:54.400
So as I said, it's quite simple.

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:57.580
It's a while loop with
four methods over here.

00:32:57.580 --> 00:33:00.280
And if you were to zoom
out and think about it,

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:04.820
it's almost like, I would
say, an agentic in silico

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:07.380
implementation of the
thought process that

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:09.340
happens in a scientist's
head when they're

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:11.940
trying to come up with
say new scientific ideas

00:33:11.940 --> 00:33:14.180
and things like that.

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:17.460
And then once you let this
computation go on for a set

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:19.580
period of time, and
that depends on when

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:20.947
you reach your end state--

00:33:20.947 --> 00:33:22.780
and again, there's a
few mechanisms in which

00:33:22.780 --> 00:33:24.000
you can reach this end state.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.340
So one way could
be like, you say

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:29.420
keep on going until you
have x number of ideas

00:33:29.420 --> 00:33:32.300
or another method could be like
you could just tell the system,

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:37.300
maybe just stop when you can
no longer solve the problem

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:39.740
or you're stuck over here.

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:43.140
So once the system reaches
that end state, what it does

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:46.540
is it visually clusters all
the ideas that it has explored

00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:50.180
so far and then puts all of them
together into this nice summary

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:54.658
doc, which in turn is then fed
back to the scientist over here.

00:33:54.658 --> 00:33:55.200
So that's it.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:56.760
So that's the crux
of the system.

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:58.460
And as I said,
again, all of these

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:02.420
are agents implemented using
our latest and greatest Gemini

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:04.660
models, so they have
all the long context

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:08.780
capabilities, the multimodal
capabilities, the agentic tool

00:34:08.780 --> 00:34:13.100
use capabilities, and everything
that we need over here.

00:34:13.100 --> 00:34:15.110
Yeah, so hopefully,
this was helpful,

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:16.810
but maybe I'll stop
over here right now

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:18.570
and maybe take a few
questions, because I

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:21.370
thought that was quite meaty.

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:22.210
Anyone?

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:23.489
Yes.

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:25.889
How is the reward systems Go?

00:34:25.889 --> 00:34:31.370
How would you evaluate whether
a hypothesis is good or not?

00:34:31.370 --> 00:34:35.170
Even for scientists, different
people have different views.

00:34:35.170 --> 00:34:37.698
How this agent system
could be evaluate

00:34:37.698 --> 00:34:40.130
what's better [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:40.130 --> 00:34:42.449
So that's the next
half of the talk.

00:34:42.449 --> 00:34:44.730
But-- yeah.

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:50.170
25 different, but the only
co-scientist [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:52.370
in the 25 preprint--

00:34:52.370 --> 00:34:55.290
co-scientist [INAUDIBLE]
system didn't really

00:34:55.290 --> 00:34:57.810
see much performance,
saturation, or plateauing

00:34:57.810 --> 00:34:59.110
with test time compute.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:02.730
Have you explored the scaling
of that further [INAUDIBLE]?

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:04.170
Yeah.

00:35:04.170 --> 00:35:06.450
So yeah, we can--

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:07.130
sorry.

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:10.630
We can discuss that
in more detail.

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:14.210
So I think it comes down
to the class of problems

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.250
that you're working with.

00:35:16.250 --> 00:35:19.290
So there's a class of problems
where the search space is

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:23.210
so big that if you
keep on throwing more

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:26.270
compute at the problem and
let the problem intelligently

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:29.250
explore the search space, then
it is very likely to come up

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:31.610
with better solutions.

00:35:31.610 --> 00:35:35.090
So for those classes of
problems, essentially,

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:37.890
there's in some ways no limits.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:39.770
So these classes of
problems are where you're

00:35:39.770 --> 00:35:41.330
trying to optimize something.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:44.150
So if you have a well-defined
fitness function,

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:45.850
then you can keep
on, in some ways,

00:35:45.850 --> 00:35:47.830
optimizing it and making
it better and better,

00:35:47.830 --> 00:35:49.890
as long as you
have more compute.

00:35:49.890 --> 00:35:51.390
So for those
classes of problems,

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:53.557
we essentially do not see
much saturation over here.

00:35:53.557 --> 00:35:55.570
So you can keep on
pushing the system.

00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:59.490
But then there are going to be,
say, two classes of problems

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:01.050
that both extremes.

00:36:01.050 --> 00:36:05.810
So there's one class where
the problem is so easy

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.450
that there's no point
in spending so much

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:09.530
compute over here.

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:13.130
Why do you want to spend,
say, millions of tokens when

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:15.770
it's just such a
trivially easy problem.

00:36:15.770 --> 00:36:18.250
So it could be some retrieval
problem or something

00:36:18.250 --> 00:36:20.435
that's extremely simple.

00:36:20.435 --> 00:36:21.810
And then at the
other extreme are

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:25.750
problems that we just don't have
the knowledge today to solve.

00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:28.970
For example, if you ask
the system to do, say,

00:36:28.970 --> 00:36:31.610
build out a time machine, it's
not going to be able to do this.

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:35.190
So no matter how much compute
you throw at the system,

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:36.738
it's not going to
be able to do this.

00:36:36.738 --> 00:36:38.530
But the good thing is
there's a large class

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:40.010
of problems in the middle.

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:42.870
So over there, you keep
on throwing more compute.

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:45.110
You keep on introducing
new information.

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:47.912
And essentially, you see this
very nice scaling property

00:36:47.912 --> 00:36:49.370
where the system
is able to come up

00:36:49.370 --> 00:36:50.770
with better and
better hypotheses

00:36:50.770 --> 00:36:52.730
over a period of time.

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:53.230
Yes.

00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:58.330
[INAUDIBLE] actual
[INAUDIBLE] what

00:36:58.330 --> 00:37:01.050
types of science, all of
these different categories

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:04.630
not made up ones like a time
machine or it's a chemistry?

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:06.570
Yeah, so that's the rest--

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:09.090
I'll show you a few
validation examples

00:37:09.090 --> 00:37:12.830
where we applied the system.

00:37:16.138 --> 00:37:18.610
[INAUDIBLE] seems
like a very linear way

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:21.050
of scientific progress,
and I hope that falsify it.

00:37:21.050 --> 00:37:26.410
And I wanted actual science
is can argue anarchy.

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:29.810
So we have a system where
you get a bunch of PhD theses

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:31.432
automatically, but
you never actually

00:37:31.432 --> 00:37:33.390
get to the next level of
having a breakthrough.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:35.953
There's a bunch of filling
the gaps, like research.

00:37:35.953 --> 00:37:37.370
So how do you still
keep the human

00:37:37.370 --> 00:37:39.490
in the loop to be
informed enough

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:41.570
to actually make the
breakthrough if that's

00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:44.650
the [INAUDIBLE] achieve, or do
you not believe in that feeling?

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:47.970
Yeah, I think that's a very
good question over here.

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:50.610
So I think one of
the goals that we

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:56.410
have is to generate such
compelling ideas or hypotheses

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:58.610
that the human who is in
the loop, the expert who

00:37:58.610 --> 00:38:01.730
is in the loop, they would
be kind of willing to drop

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:05.050
all their ideas that they
have and work on this one.

00:38:05.050 --> 00:38:08.650
And if you're able to do
that enough number of times,

00:38:08.650 --> 00:38:10.630
then that means that
your system is working.

00:38:10.630 --> 00:38:15.330
And the hope is that leads to
more discoveries and hopefully

00:38:15.330 --> 00:38:16.930
breakthroughs.

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:17.510
So yeah.

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:21.210
So that's the goal in some
ways, to just develop things

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:24.050
that scientists did
not think about.

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:26.490
And yeah, maybe all
of them don't pan out.

00:38:26.490 --> 00:38:30.250
They don't work out because
that's the nature of science.

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:33.930
But yeah, the idea is to
keep pushing and generating

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:38.970
such compelling ideas that
scientists are incentivized

00:38:38.970 --> 00:38:41.050
to go and try them.

00:38:41.050 --> 00:38:42.670
But you're spot on.

00:38:42.670 --> 00:38:46.970
I think we'll soon
enter this era,

00:38:46.970 --> 00:38:48.570
or maybe we're
already there, where

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:51.677
there's going to be so
many compelling hypotheses

00:38:51.677 --> 00:38:54.010
that actually the bottleneck
will be in the verification

00:38:54.010 --> 00:38:56.570
and validation over here.

00:38:56.570 --> 00:39:00.710
So that's the next step,
how do we prioritize them?

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:03.290
How do we allocate the
right amount of resources

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:05.650
and pick the best
ones for validation?

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:06.970
Sorry.

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:11.360
All these [INAUDIBLE] there
