00:49:53.740 --> 00:49:56.583
assumed was that
most of these plant

00:49:56.583 --> 00:49:58.500
immunoproteins-- they're
called resistasomes--

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:02.060
they'll have this
hexagonal shape,

00:50:02.060 --> 00:50:06.520
whereas what this new
insight or this new analysis,

00:50:06.520 --> 00:50:09.260
what it uncovered, was they're
actually much more novel

00:50:09.260 --> 00:50:11.400
and massive immunoproteins.

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:12.880
So it's called 11-mer over here.

00:50:12.880 --> 00:50:16.700
Essentially, that's
quite wild in some sense.

00:50:16.700 --> 00:50:20.280
So this was, again, a preprint
that came out a few weeks back.

00:50:20.280 --> 00:50:23.540
And I think this has quite
massive implications in terms

00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.860
of decoding plant
immunity and how

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:30.300
that might impact agriculture
and global food security

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:33.340
and so on and so forth.

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:34.620
This is another example.

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:37.780
So this is where we
have co-scientist

00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:39.402
using AlphaFold in the loop.

00:50:39.402 --> 00:50:40.860
And what we have
it doing over here

00:50:40.860 --> 00:50:44.620
is to design these
better Oct4 proteins.

00:50:44.620 --> 00:50:47.780
So Oct4 is one of those
classic Yamanaka factors.

00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:51.900
So these were the set of
transcription factors for which

00:50:51.900 --> 00:50:54.500
Professor Yamanaka was
awarded the Nobel Prize back

00:50:54.500 --> 00:50:57.140
in, I think, 2010
or 2011 over here.

00:50:57.140 --> 00:50:59.340
But the thing with these
transcription factors

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:02.640
is, while they
rejuvenate the cell,

00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:06.960
they can also cause tumors
and things like that.

00:51:06.960 --> 00:51:10.300
So you want to have better
versions of these proteins that

00:51:10.300 --> 00:51:14.020
have the same activity but
are more safe over here.

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:16.560
So that's what we
ask the system to do.

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:18.380
So we asked it to
predict and design

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:21.140
better sequences that
have the right activity

00:51:21.140 --> 00:51:22.980
and the right
delivery properties

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:25.260
and so on and so forth.

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:28.080
So we asked it to use
AlphaFold in a loop.

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:29.140
And that's what it does.

00:51:29.140 --> 00:51:31.960
So it makes a prediction
of a sequence.

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:35.460
It uses AlphaFold to look
at the structural stability

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:37.840
and then uses that to
refine it hypotheses.

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:40.580
And it does this in
silico a bunch of times.

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:42.580
And then what we observe
is the final sequence

00:51:42.580 --> 00:51:45.005
that emerges that actually
has the right kind of activity

00:51:45.005 --> 00:51:47.380
that we're looking for over
here and the right properties

00:51:47.380 --> 00:51:49.175
that we're looking
for over here.

00:51:49.175 --> 00:51:50.800
So we're doing a lot
of this right now.

00:51:50.800 --> 00:51:52.883
We're taking and testing
all these de novo protein

00:51:52.883 --> 00:51:54.820
designs in the lab,
and they're all looking

00:51:54.820 --> 00:51:58.940
extremely promising right now.

00:51:58.940 --> 00:52:00.480
So this is another example.

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:03.060
So this one's unpublished yet.

00:52:03.060 --> 00:52:05.300
But over here, what we
ask the system to do

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:07.400
was to go read all
the literature,

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:12.600
look at all the data, and
then predict these new, say,

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:15.620
genetic factors or these
secreted proteins that

00:52:15.620 --> 00:52:20.260
might actually take a cell
and rejuvenate it or reduce

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:22.800
the number of senescence
cells over here.

00:52:22.800 --> 00:52:25.260
So senescence essentially
means your cell essentially

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:28.860
becomes so inflamed that
it doesn't divide anymore,

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:30.440
and it causes
disease essentially.

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:32.940
So you want to reduce the number
of such cells in your body

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:35.940
and then hopefully you can
reverse them back and make

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:38.420
them like younger and
be more reflective

00:52:38.420 --> 00:52:40.595
of younger
individuals over here.

00:52:40.595 --> 00:52:42.220
So that was what the
task that we gave.

00:52:42.220 --> 00:52:44.900
So we told the system,
go find these novel

00:52:44.900 --> 00:52:48.720
or unstudied genetic
factors and things like that

00:52:48.720 --> 00:52:50.500
and just give us a few.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:51.720
So that's what it did.

00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:52.920
So it ran for a few days.

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:54.495
It gave us a list.

00:52:54.495 --> 00:52:56.620
So we tested like a few of
them, and we compared it

00:52:56.620 --> 00:52:59.220
with this positive
control, like Klotho,

00:52:59.220 --> 00:53:01.020
which is very well studied.

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:04.620
And essentially, we see that
the AI-nominated factors,

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:07.260
they actually have the same
fold reduction in terms

00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:11.700
of the number of percentage
of senescent cells over here

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:14.220
compared to this
positive control.

00:53:14.220 --> 00:53:16.900
So essentially, if
there's a plays out--

00:53:16.900 --> 00:53:19.820
we have to do more
experiments over here--

00:53:19.820 --> 00:53:22.720
we might have some net
new rejuvenation factors.

00:53:22.720 --> 00:53:24.700
And that would, again,
be extremely cool

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:26.280
if there's-- works out.

00:53:29.340 --> 00:53:31.740
Yeah, and then maybe
another example

00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:33.500
that I would want
to very quickly walk

00:53:33.500 --> 00:53:39.060
you through is a case study
related to Alzheimer's disease.

00:53:39.060 --> 00:53:43.140
So this is similar to the
antimicrobial resistance example

00:53:43.140 --> 00:53:46.900
that I showed before, which
was also a recapitulation.

00:53:46.900 --> 00:53:49.610
But over here, it's a bit
more complex in the sense

00:53:49.610 --> 00:53:53.130
that there were certain
researchers at Mass General

00:53:53.130 --> 00:53:54.352
Hospital.

00:53:54.352 --> 00:53:55.810
And what they were
trying to do was

00:53:55.810 --> 00:54:02.150
they were trying to study this
linkage between ACE inhibitors,

00:54:02.150 --> 00:54:04.130
which are, again,
very commonly used

00:54:04.130 --> 00:54:07.210
to treat blood pressure
and heart diseases

00:54:07.210 --> 00:54:08.520
and things like that.

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:10.770
So essentially, they found
this very interesting thing

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:14.890
that people who are taking
these ACE inhibitors,

00:54:14.890 --> 00:54:18.753
they actually have a heightened
risk for Alzheimer's over here.

00:54:18.753 --> 00:54:20.170
So what they wanted
to do was they

00:54:20.170 --> 00:54:23.410
wanted to understand
why is that reason.

00:54:23.410 --> 00:54:26.610
So they did years of
experimental work,

00:54:26.610 --> 00:54:29.610
and they were able to
come up with a mechanism

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:30.650
and a prediction.

00:54:30.650 --> 00:54:35.730
So it had a very complex
nine-step cascade over here.

00:54:35.730 --> 00:54:37.863
So what we asked the
AI to do was to--

00:54:37.863 --> 00:54:39.030
we gave it the same problem.

00:54:39.030 --> 00:54:41.890
And we asked it, what do you
think is the reason why ACE

00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:46.210
inhibitors lead to increased
risk of Alzheimer's?

00:54:46.210 --> 00:54:49.690
And essentially
what it did was it

00:54:49.690 --> 00:54:53.050
recapitulated their entire
nine-step mechanistic cascade

00:54:53.050 --> 00:54:54.370
over here.

00:54:54.370 --> 00:54:57.170
But the cool bit was
it was not only that.

00:54:57.170 --> 00:54:59.690
It also predicted
one of the key steps

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:02.250
that the scientists
had actually missed.

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:04.510
So again, to
simplify this a lot,

00:55:04.510 --> 00:55:10.210
essentially the thing is, so
if you take ACE inhibitors,

00:55:10.210 --> 00:55:14.570
they modulate this chemical
signal called bradykinin.

00:55:14.570 --> 00:55:15.970
And that, in turn, leads to--

00:55:19.130 --> 00:55:22.490
there's a receptor on the
surface of your brain cells

00:55:22.490 --> 00:55:23.850
called this B2R receptor.

00:55:23.850 --> 00:55:27.510
So it triggers
this B2R receptor.

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:31.650
And that, in turn, leads to
neurodegeneration over here.

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:35.210
So essentially, what the AI
said was that the missing link

00:55:35.210 --> 00:55:38.210
was essentially this
link between bradykinin

00:55:38.210 --> 00:55:40.330
and this B2R receptor.

00:55:40.330 --> 00:55:42.730
So what these
researchers then did was

00:55:42.730 --> 00:55:44.370
they went back to
the lab, and they

00:55:44.370 --> 00:55:47.010
did this is a protein
stability assay, which is

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:48.790
called a chase assay over here.

00:55:48.790 --> 00:55:52.310
And essentially, that missing
step was also, again, validated.

00:55:52.310 --> 00:55:54.930
So AI was not only
able to recapitulate

00:55:54.930 --> 00:55:56.770
what they had exactly
done, but it was also

00:55:56.770 --> 00:55:58.930
able to fill in the
finer details over here

00:55:58.930 --> 00:56:01.010
and complete their hypotheses.

00:56:01.010 --> 00:56:04.250
And this is actually
a good example

00:56:04.250 --> 00:56:08.730
of how this agentic scaffold is
actually better than baselines,

00:56:08.730 --> 00:56:12.170
because what we did was we also
gave the same problem to Claude

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:13.930
and GPT-5.

00:56:13.930 --> 00:56:15.690
And essentially, while
both those systems

00:56:15.690 --> 00:56:19.130
were able to get this first step
and the high-level hypotheses

00:56:19.130 --> 00:56:22.130
right, they were actually not
able to get the full details

00:56:22.130 --> 00:56:23.450
over here.

00:56:23.450 --> 00:56:26.330
So you really needed
this agentic harness

00:56:26.330 --> 00:56:29.850
to be able to arrive at the
key details and the specifics

00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:31.010
over here.

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:33.690
So that's again-- it's telling
you how this agentic harness is

00:56:33.690 --> 00:56:34.850
actually helpful.

00:56:34.850 --> 00:56:37.290
The time that you're
spending on verification,

00:56:37.290 --> 00:56:39.650
coming up with the right
kind of hypotheses,

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:42.930
that is how you get to much
better results and genuinely

00:56:42.930 --> 00:56:47.730
novel scientific
insights and discoveries.

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:51.870
And yeah, the last one I want to
very quickly highlight-- again,

00:56:51.870 --> 00:56:54.250
one of the things that
I do, besides trying

00:56:54.250 --> 00:56:56.810
to help improve the
system, is actually just

00:56:56.810 --> 00:56:59.170
ask it interesting questions.

00:56:59.170 --> 00:57:02.210
So one of the things that
I've been interested in

00:57:02.210 --> 00:57:05.850
is this relationship between
neurodegenerative diseases

00:57:05.850 --> 00:57:07.170
and cancer.

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:11.910
It's actually well known that if
you are an Alzheimer's patient,

00:57:11.910 --> 00:57:14.930
you actually have much
lower risk of cancer.

00:57:14.930 --> 00:57:18.250
So that's not
super well studied.

00:57:18.250 --> 00:57:21.490
So what I asked the system
was to just go and read up

00:57:21.490 --> 00:57:24.170
the literature and
then tell me, what

00:57:24.170 --> 00:57:27.810
are maybe some
understudied pathways

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:31.530
that are very common in neuro
diseases, like neuro genes,

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:35.970
essentially, that might actually
be implicated in cancer as well?

00:57:35.970 --> 00:57:39.330
So just tell me, these genes and
these specific forms of cancer

00:57:39.330 --> 00:57:41.810
that we should go and look at.

00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:43.010
So when I gave this prompt--

00:57:43.010 --> 00:57:44.843
and this is the inverse
comorbidity problem.

00:57:44.843 --> 00:57:46.490
So you have one
disease, and that

00:57:46.490 --> 00:57:48.370
just reduces the
chance of you having

00:57:48.370 --> 00:57:50.012
another disease over here.

00:57:50.012 --> 00:57:51.970
And essentially, if you
were to identify enough

00:57:51.970 --> 00:57:53.490
of these pathways,
they could also

00:57:53.490 --> 00:57:59.650
be targets to build drugs for
those specific indications.

00:57:59.650 --> 00:58:01.730
So I gave the
system this prompt.

00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:07.150
And it came up with a
pair of genes over here,

00:58:07.150 --> 00:58:10.250
which are common neuro genes.

00:58:10.250 --> 00:58:15.010
So there was these two
genes called DHX9 and SRRM4.

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:17.810
And not only that,
it also told me,

00:58:17.810 --> 00:58:20.010
if you want to
validate this gene,

00:58:20.010 --> 00:58:22.210
you should reach out
to this professor

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:25.850
Dr. Filippo Bellagio
based in Germany.

00:58:25.850 --> 00:58:27.470
So that's what I ended up doing.

00:58:27.470 --> 00:58:30.250
So I just took the hypotheses,
and I wrote a cold email

00:58:30.250 --> 00:58:31.890
to this professor.

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:36.690
And what he did was, he had a
bunch of whole genome CRISPR

00:58:36.690 --> 00:58:37.533
screen data.

00:58:37.533 --> 00:58:38.950
And he went back
and looked at it.

00:58:38.950 --> 00:58:40.650
And essentially,
what he saw was that

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:42.730
in the context of small
cell lung cancer, which

00:58:42.730 --> 00:58:45.010
is what the system
predicted, these two genes

00:58:45.010 --> 00:58:47.290
were way overexpressed
compared to other forms

00:58:47.290 --> 00:58:48.350
of cancer over here.

00:58:48.350 --> 00:58:51.130
So essentially, the
hypothesis was right.

00:58:51.130 --> 00:58:52.790
So again, that's
another cool example.

00:58:52.790 --> 00:58:54.530
It's unvalidated,
but it's essentially

00:58:54.530 --> 00:58:58.090
the system like making a
connection and surfacing it.

00:58:58.090 --> 00:59:00.370
And that's leading
to new insights

00:59:00.370 --> 00:59:04.190
and potentially discoveries
and breakthroughs.

00:59:04.190 --> 00:59:07.010
So I'll maybe stop on this note.

00:59:07.010 --> 00:59:11.790
So you can see some of the
results from the system.

00:59:11.790 --> 00:59:15.290
So this is a snapshot of the
hypotheses that I generated.

00:59:15.290 --> 00:59:18.970
Again, these reports are super
detailed, like 100-plus pages.

00:59:18.970 --> 00:59:21.410
But you can see that it's all
nicely explained with images

00:59:21.410 --> 00:59:23.130
and things like that.

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:25.122
And again, this is one
of the research contacts

00:59:25.122 --> 00:59:25.830
that I mentioned.

00:59:25.830 --> 00:59:28.710
It tells if you want to
validate this hypothesis,

00:59:28.710 --> 00:59:30.468
you may want to reach
out to these people.

00:59:30.468 --> 00:59:33.010
And it also tells you, what are
some of the unexpected things

00:59:33.010 --> 00:59:36.310
that it discovered over here?

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:38.750
So that's the co-scientist part.

00:59:38.750 --> 00:59:41.800
And maybe I can stop over here
and take a couple of questions

00:59:41.800 --> 00:59:42.580
if you have any.

00:59:45.880 --> 00:59:48.500
Have you tried playing around
with knowledge cutoffs at all.

00:59:48.500 --> 00:59:50.960
So like talkie has
been getting press

00:59:50.960 --> 00:59:53.100
lately, 13 billion
parameter model,

00:59:53.100 --> 00:59:56.260
but only on pre-1931 text.

00:59:56.260 --> 00:59:59.700
Can you predict stuff that
happened after the cutoff?

00:59:59.700 --> 01:00:01.280
Yeah.

01:00:01.280 --> 01:00:02.577
That's a good question.

01:00:02.577 --> 01:00:04.160
So I think the
imperial example that I

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:06.380
showed was exactly one of them.

01:00:06.380 --> 01:00:08.480
And even the
Alzheimer's one as well.

01:00:08.480 --> 01:00:09.600
[INAUDIBLE]

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:12.080
Yeah, I think it's
a bit difficult

01:00:12.080 --> 01:00:15.480
to isolate corpuses like
that because there's

01:00:15.480 --> 01:00:16.420
so much leakage--

01:00:16.420 --> 01:00:17.160
Yeah, definitely.

01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:19.837
--that's happening.

01:00:19.837 --> 01:00:21.920
But one thing that we've
been exciting about doing

01:00:21.920 --> 01:00:24.800
is, we're using the system
to predict future events that

01:00:24.800 --> 01:00:25.680
might happen.

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.200
So for example, there's a
bunch of clinical trials

01:00:28.200 --> 01:00:31.000
that are happening, predict
the outcomes of them.

01:00:31.000 --> 01:00:33.403
And it has reasonable
success so far.

01:00:33.403 --> 01:00:35.320
But again, it doesn't
have all the information

01:00:35.320 --> 01:00:38.320
because a lot of this is
proprietary in nature.

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:41.240
So yeah, my intuition
is that if you

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:44.360
have the right kind of knowledge
but not fully revealing

01:00:44.360 --> 01:00:47.140
the details, then this
could be extremely powerful

01:00:47.140 --> 01:00:49.140
predicting clinical trials
and things like that.

01:00:49.140 --> 01:00:51.520
And that could be very helpful.

01:00:51.520 --> 01:00:55.560
But yeah, doing the experiment
that you're talking about put--

01:00:55.560 --> 01:00:58.460
yeah, that's much harder
because of the leakage issues.

01:01:02.080 --> 01:01:05.203
How do we take more
scientific system [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:05.203 --> 01:01:07.120
Because in the end, you
have more papers like,

01:01:07.120 --> 01:01:09.178
peer reviews already
[INAUDIBLE] takes too long.

01:01:09.178 --> 01:01:10.720
And ultimately,
papers are only going

01:01:10.720 --> 01:01:12.060
to be read by other agents.

01:01:12.060 --> 01:01:14.480
So isn't there a
better way to have

01:01:14.480 --> 01:01:18.320
peer review system and a
knowledge base for other agents

01:01:18.320 --> 01:01:20.400
to work on this, rather
than waiting, like,

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:24.840
nine months before [INAUDIBLE]
test and the science?

01:01:24.840 --> 01:01:25.900
It's a great question.

01:01:25.900 --> 01:01:29.340
And honestly, I don't
have a good answer to it.

01:01:29.340 --> 01:01:33.020
We're obviously starting to see
a lot of AI usage in papers.

01:01:33.020 --> 01:01:37.280
And I think it was just this
morning where arXiv came up

01:01:37.280 --> 01:01:41.052
with this new policy that, if
you have hallucinated references

01:01:41.052 --> 01:01:42.760
in your papers, then
they would block you

01:01:42.760 --> 01:01:45.160
from using arXiv for a year.

01:01:45.160 --> 01:01:48.260
But I don't think that's very
productive, in my opinion.

01:01:48.260 --> 01:01:51.040
I think we have to
have better mechanisms.

01:01:51.040 --> 01:01:53.160
And again, people are
using AI and peer reviews

01:01:53.160 --> 01:01:55.160
and that can be helpful.

01:01:55.160 --> 01:01:59.560
But the risk is, again, that if
you don't use it judiciously,

01:01:59.560 --> 01:02:02.400
then you're going to favor only
certain kind of papers that

01:02:02.400 --> 01:02:04.920
pass through this
filter or funnel,

01:02:04.920 --> 01:02:09.600
and then you leave the
other topics unexplored.

01:02:09.600 --> 01:02:11.200
So yeah, I think
so that is going

01:02:11.200 --> 01:02:15.680
to be like an important
challenge over here.

01:02:15.680 --> 01:02:18.022
Again, I don't
have a good answer.

01:02:18.022 --> 01:02:19.480
I think there's
also things that we

01:02:19.480 --> 01:02:23.210
can do to enable agents to share
the discoveries and information.

01:02:23.210 --> 01:02:24.960
Maybe there's a need
for a science context

01:02:24.960 --> 01:02:26.120
protocol over here.

01:02:26.120 --> 01:02:29.680
There's need for formats
that can help you standardize

01:02:29.680 --> 01:02:32.160
scientific data and how
they are generated, how they

01:02:32.160 --> 01:02:35.880
are shared and decentralized.

01:02:35.880 --> 01:02:38.480
So yeah, I mean, there could be
a lot of cool engineering work

01:02:38.480 --> 01:02:39.188
that we could do.

01:02:39.188 --> 01:02:42.960
But again, there are going to be
some thorny issues also that we

01:02:42.960 --> 01:02:45.382
have to address with the
flood of papers that's

01:02:45.382 --> 01:02:46.840
coming because
humans are not going

01:02:46.840 --> 01:02:48.460
to be able to keep up with it.

01:02:51.040 --> 01:02:52.740
Any other questions?

01:02:52.740 --> 01:02:53.760
Yes.

01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:57.640
So for the reports,
[INAUDIBLE] you mentioned that

01:02:57.640 --> 01:02:59.140
around 100 pages.

01:02:59.140 --> 01:03:01.120
Yeah, I just wonder
how, I guess,

01:03:01.120 --> 01:03:05.180
humans going through the
[? doors ?] and trusting

01:03:05.180 --> 01:03:06.113
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:03:12.040 --> 01:03:13.040
Yeah.

01:03:13.040 --> 01:03:16.200
So one thing we try
to do is-- again,

01:03:16.200 --> 01:03:19.400
we want to have all the
details in the reports.

01:03:19.400 --> 01:03:21.920
But we also tell
the scientists where

01:03:21.920 --> 01:03:23.920
to spend time and attention on.

01:03:23.920 --> 01:03:26.960
So we tell, maybe
this is like the most

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:29.400
compelling or
interesting hypothesis,

01:03:29.400 --> 01:03:31.640
so you may want to
read them first.

01:03:31.640 --> 01:03:34.280
But again, it's also
possible that the system

01:03:34.280 --> 01:03:36.840
is too conservative.

01:03:36.840 --> 01:03:39.240
So even if it thinks that
certain hypotheses are not

01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:42.360
viable, they may be still
having some interesting clues

01:03:42.360 --> 01:03:45.440
or some interesting insights
that might help the scientist.

01:03:45.440 --> 01:03:48.123
So that's why we still
keep them in the report.

01:03:48.123 --> 01:03:49.540
But we clearly
tell the scientist,

01:03:49.540 --> 01:03:52.040
you should focus your energies
on this particular section

01:03:52.040 --> 01:03:53.840
or this particular
path because that seems

01:03:53.840 --> 01:03:55.640
like the most promising one.

01:03:55.640 --> 01:03:58.040
And on the flip side, there
might be certain explorations

01:03:58.040 --> 01:04:00.160
where the system just
comes back and says,

01:04:00.160 --> 01:04:02.840
none of the ideas that I have
so far are going to work.

01:04:02.840 --> 01:04:05.000
So you may want to
reframe the problem

01:04:05.000 --> 01:04:09.880
or maybe focus your attention
on a subproblem and things

01:04:09.880 --> 01:04:10.513
like that.

01:04:10.513 --> 01:04:12.680
So that happens quite a bit
in mathematical problems

01:04:12.680 --> 01:04:15.920
over here where if you
make it solve the full--

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:18.605
come up with the full
proof, then it breaks down.

01:04:18.605 --> 01:04:20.480
But if you break it down
into the right steps

01:04:20.480 --> 01:04:23.280
and the right chunks and have
the human work iteratively

01:04:23.280 --> 01:04:26.800
and interact with it, then it's
able to make progress over here.

01:04:26.800 --> 01:04:29.920
So again, we have all those
mechanisms set up in the system

01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:30.980
to be able to do that.

01:04:34.110 --> 01:04:35.807
For the case that you
just talked about,

01:04:35.807 --> 01:04:37.390
you mentioned you
can run it overnight

01:04:37.390 --> 01:04:41.310
or over a couple of
nights-- how many 3.1 Pro

01:04:41.310 --> 01:04:44.790
or whatever tokens for
magnitude [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:44.790 --> 01:04:48.230
how many agents there
are in parallel?

01:04:48.230 --> 01:04:51.330
Yeah, maybe I'll tell you
once this is not recorded.

01:04:54.510 --> 01:04:56.630
Any other-- yes.

01:04:56.630 --> 01:04:58.936
You guys are just thinking
about [INAUDIBLE],

01:04:58.936 --> 01:05:01.910
like in terms of bad actors
that might have [INAUDIBLE]?

01:05:01.910 --> 01:05:03.270
Yeah, it's a good question.

01:05:03.270 --> 01:05:05.630
So yeah, we have
different layers

01:05:05.630 --> 01:05:08.575
of safety checks over here.

01:05:08.575 --> 01:05:09.950
So there's one
that happens right

01:05:09.950 --> 01:05:13.150
when the scientist is specifying
the prompt or the goal.

01:05:13.150 --> 01:05:15.550
So over there, we
check and make sure

01:05:15.550 --> 01:05:19.270
that it's not
something nefarious.

01:05:19.270 --> 01:05:21.170
But oftentimes, you
can get around that.

01:05:21.170 --> 01:05:23.750
You can phrase it
in different ways.

01:05:23.750 --> 01:05:30.070
So what we also do is we
also monitor the ideas

01:05:30.070 --> 01:05:32.630
that it's continuously producing
and the exploration that's

01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:33.250
happening.

01:05:33.250 --> 01:05:35.710
And again, if we see, say,
a percentage of the ideas

01:05:35.710 --> 01:05:39.370
are getting into this unsafe
territory, and that's right now,

01:05:39.370 --> 01:05:42.250
I think, set to 10%
of the ideas, again,

01:05:42.250 --> 01:05:44.910
we halt the computation
and we tell the scientists

01:05:44.910 --> 01:05:48.150
that there's an unsafe
research goal, essentially.

01:05:48.150 --> 01:05:51.230
And then finally, again,
because these agents are all

01:05:51.230 --> 01:05:54.790
based on Gemini, and that itself
has CBR and testing and things

01:05:54.790 --> 01:05:56.430
like that has
happened, so we are

01:05:56.430 --> 01:06:00.510
able to get some
of those properties

01:06:00.510 --> 01:06:02.510
from within the
base model itself.

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:05.110
But yeah, you have
a multiagent setup,

01:06:05.110 --> 01:06:09.790
the surface of where
things could go wrong

01:06:09.790 --> 01:06:13.030
and where things could be used
in an improper manner, that

01:06:13.030 --> 01:06:14.710
also expands quite a bit.

01:06:14.710 --> 01:06:17.310
So we have to have this
multilayered approach

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:20.348
to safety essentially.

01:06:20.348 --> 01:06:22.890
Unfortunately, I think that's
all the time we have for today.

01:06:22.890 --> 01:06:25.050
But round of applause
for our amazing speaker.

01:06:25.050 --> 01:06:28.400
[APPLAUSE]

